; modules.loon — Module system guide
[fn guide-modules-page []
  [doc-page
    "Modules"
    "File-based modules with pub visibility."
    
    [doc-section
      "File = Module"
      [doc-text
        "Loon's module system holds one idea and no more: every "
        [doc-code ".loon"]
        " file is a module, and its name is the file name with the extension dropped. No module declarations, no manifests, no boilerplate. Write a file called "
        [doc-code "greet.loon"]
        " and you have a module called "
        [doc-code "greet"]
        "."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "; greet.loon\n"
          "[fn hello [] \"hi\"]\n"
          "[pub fn world [] \"world\"]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Everything in a module is private until you say otherwise. The "
        [doc-code "hello"]
        " function above is invisible from outside; only "
        [doc-code "world"]
        ", marked "
        [doc-code "pub"]
        ", is reachable by other modules. Encapsulation costs you nothing, and your internal helpers stay internal."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Pub Exports"
      [doc-text
        "To let something out of its module, write "
        [doc-code "pub"]
        " before the definition. It works on functions, bindings, and types alike."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[pub fn add [a b] [+ a b]]\n"
          "[pub let version \"0.1.0\"]\n"
          "[pub type Color\n"
          "  Red Green Blue]"]]
      [doc-text
        [doc-code "pub"]
        " is the front door of your module. Everything behind it is an implementation detail you are free to change without breaking anyone. The "
        [doc-code "pub"]
        " items, and only those, are your module's contract with the world."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Use / Import"
      [doc-text
        "To bring a module into scope, reach for the "
        [doc-code "use"]
        " keyword. Once imported, you call its exports through dot syntax."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str "[use math]\n" "[println [math.add 1 2]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "The module name is the file name, so "
        [doc-code "[use math]"]
        " looks for "
        [doc-code "math.loon"]
        ". Predictable, and self-explaining: there is no configuration file to comb through to learn where a module lives."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Aliasing"
      [doc-text
        "Module names are sometimes long, or they collide with something already in scope. Rename one on import with "
        [doc-code "as"]
        "."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str
          "[use string-utils as str-u]\n"
          "[println [str-u.capitalize \"hello\"]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Once aliased, the original name is gone from scope; only the alias answers. The result stays unambiguous, and you get to choose the name that reads best where you are."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Selective Imports"
      [doc-text
        "When you need only a few names from a module, pull them straight into scope. No dot prefix required."]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str "[use math [add subtract]]\n" "[println [add 1 2]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "This earns its place when you lean on a function often and the "
        [doc-code "module.function"]
        " spelling starts to drag. Take care not to import names that collide with your own."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Directory Structure"
      [doc-text
        "As a project grows, you will reach for directories to keep modules in order. Nesting builds module paths, the segments joined by "
        [doc-code "/"]
        "."]
      [code-block
        {:label "STRUCTURE"}
        [str
          "src/\n"
          "  main.loon\n"
          "  math.loon\n"
          "  utils/\n"
          "    string.loon\n"
          "    io.loon"]]
      [code-block
        {:label "LOON"}
        [str "[use utils/string]\n" "[println [string.trim \"  hi  \"]]"]]
      [doc-text
        "Note the split: you import by the full path ("
        [doc-code "utils/string"]
        ") but refer to the module by its last segment ("
        [doc-code "string"]
        "). Use stays short, while the import remains explicit about where the module came from."]
      [doc-note
        "info"
        "A directory with a mod.loon file exports that file as the directory module, so utils/mod.loon lets you write [use utils] directly."]]
    
    [doc-section
      "Cycle Detection"
      [doc-text
        "Circular dependencies breed confusion and quiet bugs. Loon catches them at compile time and points to exactly where the cycle closes."]
      [code-block
        {:label "ERROR"}
        [str
          "error: circular dependency detected\n"
          "  --> a.loon:1\n"
          "  a.loon -> b.loon -> a.loon"]]
      [doc-text
        "The fix is usually clean: lift the shared code into a third module that both files import. A cycle is a sign that two modules are more entangled than they ought to be, so breaking it tends to leave the design better than it found it."]]
    
    [doc-prev-next "Effects" "/guide/effects" "Macros" "/guide/macros"]]]
